WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.185
Welcome to section twelve, respiratory anatomy, Hess chapter forty-nine.

2
00:00:06.185 --> 00:00:10.053
The study guide flags this chapter as everything.

3
00:00:10.053 --> 00:00:35.160
We will cover the five processes of respiration, fetal and postnatal lung development, upper and lower airway, the lungs and alveoli, pleurae and mediastinum, the bony thorax, respiratory muscles and their innervation, the mucociliary apparatus, airway and vascular smooth muscle, and the key immune cells of the lung.

4
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.450
Names and numbers carry this chapter, so we will keep them precise.

5
00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:48.315
Respiration has five distinct processes, and ventilation is only the first.

6
00:00:48.315 --> 00:00:53.990
One, ventilation: movement of air from the atmosphere to the lung.

7
00:00:53.990 --> 00:01:00.095
Two, perfusion: circulation of blood through the cardiovascular system.

8
00:01:00.095 --> 00:01:10.499
Three, diffusion: movement of oxygen and carbon dioxide across the alveolar-capillary membrane from high to low pressure.

9
00:01:10.499 --> 00:01:16.260
Four, external respiration: gas exchange between alveoli and blood.

10
00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:22.021
Five, internal respiration: gas exchange between blood and tissues.

11
00:01:22.021 --> 00:01:27.696
Gas exchange occurs exclusively at the acinus, the alveolar level.

12
00:01:27.696 --> 00:01:36.467
Gas distribution is gas moving through the upper airway and the conducting airways of the lower tract.

13
00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:44.704
Memorize this order: embryonic, pseudoglandular, canalicular, saccular, alveolar.

14
00:01:44.704 --> 00:01:51.963
Embryonic, weeks four to six, builds proximal airways from a lung bud off the foregut.

15
00:01:51.963 --> 00:02:04.961
Pseudoglandular, weeks six to sixteen, builds conducting airways and nearly completes the diaphragm; survival after premature delivery is highly unlikely.

16
00:02:04.961 --> 00:02:22.011
Canalicular, weeks seventeen to twenty-six, builds the vascular bed, and type one and type two cells appear; limited gas exchange becomes possible, so survival is possible with intensive, advanced care.

17
00:02:22.011 --> 00:02:30.030
Saccular, weeks twenty-seven to thirty-six, forms terminal saccules, and surfactant is present.

18
00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:38.808
Alveolar, thirty-six weeks and beyond, with alveoli peaking at about three hundred million by age eight.

19
00:02:38.808 --> 00:02:45.392
Hess's prose and glossary give different dates; the guide says use Table 49-2.

20
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:53.324
Before birth the lung is fluid-filled, which keeps it expanded and helps it grow.

21
00:02:53.324 --> 00:03:08.627
The first breath needs a transpulmonary pressure of approximately forty to eighty centimeters of water and roughly forty milliliters of volume to overcome surface tension and residual fluid.

22
00:03:08.627 --> 00:03:14.184
Capillaries and lymphatics may take several hours to clear the fluid.

23
00:03:14.184 --> 00:03:25.782
The infant has a proportionally larger head and tongue, smaller nasal passages, and a higher larynx, which make it a preferential nose breather.

24
00:03:25.782 --> 00:03:32.226
The cricoid ring is the narrowest point in the infant, the glottis in the adult.

25
00:03:32.226 --> 00:03:44.388
One millimeter of circumferential edema can cut infant airway diameter by more than fifty percent, as in croup, epiglottitis, or post-extubation edema.

26
00:03:44.388 --> 00:03:52.925
Newer imaging questions the cricoid rule, but for boards the deck says children, cricoid; adults, glottis.

27
00:03:54.333 --> 00:04:01.668
The upper tract runs from nose and mouth to larynx; the lower tract begins at the trachea.

28
00:04:01.668 --> 00:04:14.138
The nasal cavity filters, warms, and humidifies air; nasal conchae are bony ridges projecting into the cavity, and the respiratory mucosa secretes mucus.

29
00:04:14.138 --> 00:04:27.422
Paranasal sinuses, maxillary, frontal, sphenoid, and ethmoid, are hollow spaces lined with ciliated epithelium that act as resonant chambers and lighten the skull.

30
00:04:27.422 --> 00:04:45.922
The pharynx has three regions: the nasopharynx carries air only and has ciliated epithelium with goblet cells; the oropharynx has stratified squamous epithelium and carries air and food; the laryngopharynx is the most inferior.

31
00:04:45.922 --> 00:04:52.687
Enlarged tonsils, adenoids, or uvula may impede breathing, especially during sleep.

32
00:04:52.687 --> 00:04:57.658
The epiglottis covers the tracheal opening during swallowing.

33
00:04:59.067 --> 00:05:06.562
The larynx is a cartilaginous passage between pharynx and trachea, housing the vocal cords.

34
00:05:06.562 --> 00:05:12.493
The hyoid bone sits above it; the epiglottis is the uppermost cartilage.

35
00:05:12.493 --> 00:05:17.765
The thyroid cartilage is the largest and makes the Adam's apple.

36
00:05:17.765 --> 00:05:25.425
The cricoid cartilage is the only complete ring around the airway and sits below the thyroid.

37
00:05:25.425 --> 00:05:36.051
Between the thyroid and cricoid lies the cricothyroid membrane, where a cricothyrotomy can open the lower airway in an emergency.

38
00:05:36.051 --> 00:05:49.642
The vocal cords are mucosal folds; the opening between them is the glottis, a triangular slit that separates the upper and lower airway and closes during swallowing.

39
00:05:51.067 --> 00:06:10.536
The trachea is ten to thirteen centimeters long, about two to two-and-a-half centimeters in diameter, supported by sixteen to twenty C-shaped cartilages, and lined with pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium; smoking destroys its cilia.

40
00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:15.824
At the carina it bifurcates into right and left main stem bronchi.

41
00:06:15.824 --> 00:06:25.038
The right main stem leaves at twenty to thirty degrees from vertical, the left at forty-five to fifty-five degrees.

42
00:06:25.038 --> 00:06:32.490
Because the right angle is smaller, foreign bodies and suction catheters more often go right.

43
00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:45.309
For endotracheal tube placement, the deck target is four to six centimeters above the carina with the head neutral; too deep usually enters the right main stem.

44
00:06:45.309 --> 00:06:53.642
Hess chapter seventeen says three to seven centimeters, but the guide says use four to six on this exam.

45
00:06:55.067 --> 00:07:04.609
Main bronchi split by dichotomous branching into lobar, then segmental, then subsegmental bronchi, then bronchioles.

46
00:07:04.609 --> 00:07:09.627
Up to the bronchioles the airways are supported by cartilage.

47
00:07:09.627 --> 00:07:20.239
Bronchioles are less than one millimeter wide and have no cartilage; elastic forces of the lung hold them open during exhalation.

48
00:07:20.239 --> 00:07:24.516
Terminal bronchioles are the last conducting airway.

49
00:07:24.516 --> 00:07:42.367
Hess's prose places the respiratory zone at the sixteenth to twenty-third generations; the chapter's figure places terminal bronchioles at generation sixteen and the respiratory zone at seventeen through twenty-three.

50
00:07:42.367 --> 00:07:44.424
The guide says know both.

51
00:07:44.424 --> 00:07:52.732
The conducting zone distributes air; the respiratory zone, the acinus, is where gas exchange happens.

52
00:07:52.732 --> 00:08:04.167
The Mucus deck's notes credit alveolar ducts and respiratory bronchioles with about ten percent of exchange, alveoli with the other ninety.

53
00:08:05.567 --> 00:08:07.980
Pause and work this one out.

54
00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:19.441
The RCP 105 Principles of Mechanical Ventilation deck gives a quick bedside rule for estimating anatomic dead space from body weight.

55
00:08:19.441 --> 00:08:23.405
Your patient weighs one hundred eighty pounds.

56
00:08:23.405 --> 00:08:31.592
What is the rule, and about how many milliliters of anatomic dead space does this patient have?

57
00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:38.500
[6-second practice pause]

58
00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:51.831
The deck's rule is about one milliliter of anatomic dead space per pound of body weight, so a one-hundred-fifty-pound person has about one hundred fifty milliliters.

59
00:08:51.831 --> 00:09:01.006
For our patient, one hundred eighty pounds times one milliliter per pound equals about one hundred eighty milliliters.

60
00:09:01.006 --> 00:09:03.650
The unit is pounds, not kilograms.

61
00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:17.725
Remember this is an estimate from the instructor's deck, not a measured value, and it sits alongside the chapter's point that the conducting airways move air without exchanging gas.

62
00:09:19.133 --> 00:09:27.438
Normal adult lungs weigh about eight hundred grams and are about ninety percent gas and ten percent tissue.

63
00:09:27.438 --> 00:09:30.776
There are five lobes and eighteen segments.

64
00:09:30.776 --> 00:09:44.902
The right lung has three lobes, upper, middle, and lower: a horizontal fissure separates upper and middle, and an oblique fissure separates middle and lower, with ten segments total.

65
00:09:44.902 --> 00:09:56.544
The left lung has two lobes, upper and lower, separated by an oblique fissure, with eight segments, including the lingular segments of the upper lobe.

66
00:09:56.544 --> 00:10:01.900
The hilum, on the medial surface, is where bronchi and vessels enter.

67
00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:07.566
The right lung is larger because the left shares its side with the heart.

68
00:10:07.566 --> 00:10:12.533
Knowing segments matters for bronchoscopy and postural drainage.

69
00:10:13.933 --> 00:10:23.029
The adult lung has about three hundred million alveoli, each two hundred to three hundred microns in diameter.

70
00:10:23.029 --> 00:10:29.810
Pulmonary capillaries cover eighty-five to ninety percent of the alveolar surface.

71
00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:44.611
Capillary blood and alveolar gas are separated by vascular endothelium, interstitial space, and alveolar epithelium, and gas crosses this alveolar-capillary membrane by diffusion.

72
00:10:44.611 --> 00:10:52.384
The flat type one cells that line it are only zero point one to zero point five microns thick.

73
00:10:52.384 --> 00:11:02.720
Adjacent alveoli communicate through pores of Kohn, and channels of Lambert connect alveoli to adjacent terminal bronchioles.

74
00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:09.583
The COPD deck notes that this collateral ventilation helps compensate in emphysema.

75
00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:12.321
Pause and think.

76
00:11:12.321 --> 00:11:15.705
Two alveolar cell types line the air sac.

77
00:11:15.705 --> 00:11:20.575
One is thin and squamous and does most of the gas exchange.

78
00:11:20.575 --> 00:11:31.141
The other is cuboidal, sits in the corners, and makes a specific substance that keeps alveoli from collapsing at end-expiration.

79
00:11:31.141 --> 00:11:39.725
Which cell makes it, what is it called, and which disease name is tied to its deficiency in the newborn?

80
00:11:40.633 --> 00:11:46.633
[6-second practice pause]

81
00:11:47.133 --> 00:12:07.573
Type one pneumocytes are flat and squamous, cover about ninety to ninety-five percent of the alveolar surface, and are the major site of gas exchange, but they are sensitive to injury from high oxygen, bacteria, bleomycin, cyclophosphamide, and particulates.

82
00:12:07.573 --> 00:12:16.842
Type two pneumocytes are cuboidal, cover the remaining five to ten percent, and are the primary source of surfactant.

83
00:12:16.842 --> 00:12:22.150
They also differentiate into type one cells when those are damaged.

84
00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:31.340
Hess gives slightly different percentages in other passages; learn the ninety-to-ninety-five and five-to-ten ranges.

85
00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:42.510
Surfactant is ninety percent phospholipid and ten percent protein; the main surface-active molecule is dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine, DPPC.

86
00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:51.858
Absence of surfactant causes atelectasis, and surfactant deficiency is seen in neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.

87
00:12:53.267 --> 00:13:04.653
The interstitium is the space between alveolar epithelium and vascular endothelium, a continuum from the visceral pleura to the hilum.

88
00:13:04.653 --> 00:13:18.928
Its matrix holds elastin, which gives the lung its elastic recoil, and collagen, which limits over-distention and makes up fifteen to twenty percent of lung dry weight.

89
00:13:18.928 --> 00:13:25.811
Elastases or alpha-one antitrypsin deficiency can cause considerable lung damage.

90
00:13:25.811 --> 00:13:38.981
Interstitial lung disease means more cells and a thicker interstitium; the ILD deck adds that the thickened barrier reduces diffusion and causes hypoxemia.

91
00:13:38.981 --> 00:13:52.492
Lymphatics lie beneath the visceral pleura, exit at the hilum, drain to mediastinal nodes, and remove excess fluid and protein, bacteria, and foreign material.

92
00:13:53.900 --> 00:14:04.100
The visceral pleura covers the lung surface and dips into the fissures, is innervated by the vagus, and has no pain sensation.

93
00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:18.187
The parietal pleura lines the chest wall, is innervated by intercostal and phrenic nerves, and is very pain-sensitive; pleurisy pain may be referred to the neck and shoulder.

94
00:14:18.187 --> 00:14:21.182
Ciliated mesothelial cells line both.

95
00:14:21.182 --> 00:14:27.658
The pleural space holds less than twenty milliliters of thin, low-protein fluid.

96
00:14:27.658 --> 00:14:36.402
Capillary leakage is balanced by pleural lymphatics, which can handle about seven hundred milliliters a day.

97
00:14:36.402 --> 00:14:45.631
The fluid lets lung and chest wall slide while holding them tightly together, and pleural pressure stays negative.

98
00:14:45.631 --> 00:14:57.450
The mediastinum is everything in the thorax except the lungs and pleurae: heart in pericardium, great vessels, trachea, esophagus, thymus, nerves.

99
00:14:58.867 --> 00:15:07.362
The bony thorax is the sternum, twelve pairs of ribs, thoracic vertebrae, clavicles, and scapulae.

100
00:15:07.362 --> 00:15:20.019
The sternum has three parts: the manubrium connecting to ribs one and two, the body connecting to ribs three to seven, and the xiphoid at the tip.

101
00:15:20.019 --> 00:15:32.243
Ribs one to seven are important in ventilation: they lift outward and upward like a bucket handle while the sternum rises like a pump handle.

102
00:15:32.243 --> 00:15:38.744
The anteroposterior diameter is normally about half the width of the chest.

103
00:15:38.744 --> 00:15:52.442
In obstructive disease with air trapping, the AP diameter increases to produce a barrel chest; the RCP 103 decks tie it to COPD and the emphysema pink puffer.

104
00:15:53.867 --> 00:15:58.221
The diaphragm is the primary muscle of inspiration.

105
00:15:58.221 --> 00:16:07.015
It is dome-shaped, attaches to vertebrae, ribs, and xiphoid, and its fibers join at the central tendon.

106
00:16:07.015 --> 00:16:18.028
On inspiration it contracts and flattens, lowering intrathoracic pressure and creating a pressure difference with the atmosphere.

107
00:16:18.028 --> 00:16:28.615
Excursion is about one point five centimeters in tidal breathing and up to six to ten centimeters at high work of breathing.

108
00:16:28.615 --> 00:16:47.312
The phrenic nerve, C3 to C5, supplies it; Hess says cervical fractures between C1 and C5 are likely to impair breathing, and the neuro deck adds that a spinal cord injury at or above C3 to C5 can paralyze the diaphragm.

109
00:16:47.312 --> 00:16:57.813
Accessory inspiratory muscles are the scalene, sternocleidomastoid, pectoralis major, trapezius, and external intercostals.

110
00:16:57.813 --> 00:17:08.912
Accessory expiratory muscles are the internal intercostals and the abdominals: rectus, external and internal obliques, transverse.

111
00:17:08.912 --> 00:17:21.292
Exhalation is normally passive, from elastic recoil of the lungs plus relaxation of the diaphragm; abdominal contraction means active exhalation.

112
00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:24.260
A quick nerve map.

113
00:17:24.260 --> 00:17:29.978
The phrenic nerve, C3 to C5, is the motor supply to the diaphragm.

114
00:17:29.978 --> 00:17:35.870
The vagus supplies the visceral pleura, which has no pain sensation.

115
00:17:35.870 --> 00:17:43.495
Intercostal and phrenic nerves supply the parietal pleura, which is very pain-sensitive.

116
00:17:43.495 --> 00:17:50.860
The olfactory nerve carries smell, and nasal sensory neurons start the sneeze reflex.

117
00:17:50.860 --> 00:18:00.651
In the pulmonary vasculature, nerve endings release acetylcholine and norepinephrine, which affect vascular tone.

118
00:18:00.651 --> 00:18:17.200
The Mucus deck adds that submucosal glands are under parasympathetic control and increase mucus with cholinergic stimulation, while surface goblet cells do not seem to be directly innervated.

119
00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:30.401
We breathe twelve to twenty-four thousand liters of air per day, so the primary defense in the conducting airways is the mucociliary escalator.

120
00:18:30.401 --> 00:18:37.250
Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, with goblet cells, lines the airway.

121
00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:43.027
Cilia beat in a watery sol layer, and a mucous gel layer rides on top.

122
00:18:43.027 --> 00:19:05.308
Where sources differ, the guide uses the instructor's deck: a ciliated-to-goblet ratio of five to one, not Hess's four to one; cilia seven microns long in larger airways and five or less in small bronchioles, not Hess's six; and one hundred milliliters secreted per day.

123
00:19:05.308 --> 00:19:11.497
Hess adds that cilia beat one thousand to fifteen hundred times per minute.

124
00:19:11.497 --> 00:19:21.400
Dehydration limits secretion production, and smoking, cystic fibrosis, COPD, asthma, and several drugs impair transport.

125
00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:29.710
Both airways and pulmonary vessels have smooth muscle under involuntary control.

126
00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:40.076
The pulmonary vasculature has less smooth muscle than systemic arteries, and its tone, pressure, and resistance are low.

127
00:19:40.076 --> 00:19:46.036
Smooth muscle directs regional blood flow by changing vessel caliber.

128
00:19:46.036 --> 00:19:53.465
Vasoactive chemicals come from endothelial cells, nerves, mast cells, or other organs.

129
00:19:53.465 --> 00:20:04.781
Hypercapnia, a fall in pH, and particularly hypoxia cause pulmonary vasoconstriction; hypoxia acts directly on smooth muscle cells.

130
00:20:04.781 --> 00:20:12.641
This response exists to match alveolar ventilation and perfusion and maximize gas exchange.

131
00:20:12.641 --> 00:20:21.625
Note that chapter forty-nine does not cover the gross anatomy of the pulmonary or bronchial circulation.

132
00:20:23.033 --> 00:20:24.711
Three cells to know.

133
00:20:24.711 --> 00:20:38.719
Mast cells, ten to twenty microns, sit in respiratory mucosa and alveolar septa with abundant granules; they release histamine, serotonin, leukotrienes, and cytokines.

134
00:20:38.719 --> 00:20:42.158
Mast cell numbers are elevated in asthma.

135
00:20:42.158 --> 00:21:03.463
Alveolar macrophages, sometimes called type three cells, are bone-marrow derived and perform phagocytosis, antigen presentation, and cytokine production, with interleukin-one and TNF as the most significant cytokines; they remove bacteria from the acini.

136
00:21:03.463 --> 00:21:18.309
Dendritic cells are also bone-marrow derived and mobile, and their main role is antigen presentation; they take up antigens and migrate to lymph nodes to activate T lymphocytes.

137
00:21:18.309 --> 00:21:23.258
Inhaled or systemic steroids reduce dendritic cell numbers.

138
00:21:24.667 --> 00:21:28.974
The Hess chapter forty-nine key points, in brief.

139
00:21:28.974 --> 00:21:36.094
The respiratory system's primary functions are gas distribution and gas exchange.

140
00:21:36.094 --> 00:21:45.763
The five stages of fetal lung development are embryonic, pseudoglandular, canalicular, saccular, and alveolar.

141
00:21:45.763 --> 00:21:51.389
Postnatal development creates infant-adult anatomic differences.

142
00:21:51.389 --> 00:21:57.367
Upper airway: nose, nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx, larynx.

143
00:21:57.367 --> 00:22:02.905
Lower airway: trachea, main stem bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli.

144
00:22:02.905 --> 00:22:06.509
The lungs consist of airways and alveoli.

145
00:22:06.509 --> 00:22:12.222
The bony thorax is sternum, ribs, vertebrae, clavicles, scapulae.

146
00:22:12.222 --> 00:22:19.606
Respiratory muscles are diaphragm plus accessory inspiratory and expiratory muscles.

147
00:22:19.606 --> 00:22:28.221
Visceral pleura surrounds the lung, parietal lines the chest wall, with the pleural space between.

148
00:22:28.221 --> 00:22:32.528
The mucociliary apparatus clears the lower tract.

149
00:22:32.528 --> 00:22:38.154
Important lower-tract cells include mast, macrophage, dendritic.

150
00:22:38.154 --> 00:22:41.758
Alveoli have type one and type two cells.

151
00:22:41.758 --> 00:22:46.417
Interstitium lies between epithelium and endothelium.

152
00:22:47.833 --> 00:22:59.234
The lung distributes gas through the conducting zone from trachea through terminal bronchiole, then exchanges gas in the acinus.

153
00:22:59.234 --> 00:23:11.972
Right main stem is more vertical, twenty to thirty degrees; left is forty-five to fifty-five; ETT tip four to six centimeters above the carina.

154
00:23:11.972 --> 00:23:20.255
The right lung has three lobes with ten segments; the left has two lobes with eight segments.

155
00:23:20.255 --> 00:23:26.045
Type one cells line gas exchange; type two cells make surfactant.

156
00:23:26.045 --> 00:23:34.685
Diaphragm is primary, phrenic C3 to C5; accessory inspiratory muscles join with increased effort.

157
00:23:34.685 --> 00:23:38.871
Visceral pleura has no pain; parietal has pain.

158
00:23:38.871 --> 00:23:43.681
Mucociliary escalator defends; smoking destroys cilia.

159
00:23:43.681 --> 00:23:50.183
Five stages: embryonic, pseudoglandular, canalicular, saccular, alveolar.
